# Record (or clear) the software zero of one axis.
#
# The rig's counter is never zeroed in hardware -- see AxisOrigin in the server's
# common code, and DeviceConfig::enableWritePosition for why writing it is off.
# So HOME means 0 only because the counter standing at the origin sensor is
# remembered, here, and subtracted from every later reading.
#
# Call this ONLY when the axis really is on its origin sensor. An unchecked
# capture writes a zero that is wrong for every measurement afterwards, and
# nothing about the number looks wrong when it happens.
#
# axis: "x" | "y" | "z" | "yaw" | "pitch"
string axis

# "capture"        -- read the counter now and make that the zero (software)
# "clear"          -- forget this axis' zero; coordinates go back to provisional
# "write_hardware" -- send WRP to the controller, setting its own counter
#
# "write_hardware" is the one that touches the rig, and it is gated: the server
# sends nothing unless DeviceConfig::enableWritePosition is set, which it is not
# by default. No rig in this system's history has actually received a WRP -- the
# old ROS client made it a no-op and the Python's range guards were inverted so it
# always refused. Turning it on changes how the hardware behaves; it does not
# restore anything. The call is kept so the homing flow reads the same, and so
# that enabling the flag is a config decision rather than a code change.
string action

# "write_hardware" only: the counter value to write.
float64 position
---
bool success

# The coordinate after the change: "0.000" for a successful capture.
string coordinate
string message
